
 fillipinfo - Fix IP-related fields (org, country, region, hostname, email)
              in a usage or user table.

 Looks up missing or stale IP-related info using the ipinfo and geoip2
 modules and updates records in the chosen table.

 USAGE:
   fillipinfo [-b] [-f] -t TableName
              (-d Dates | -m YearMonths | -N NumberDays | -y Years)

 OPTIONS:

   -t TableName   (required)
        Table to fix. One of:
           ipinfo, wuser, allusage, codusage, tdsusage, osdfusage, awsusage

        Or 'all', to do every one of them in that order, which is the order
        they have to be done in: ipinfo first, the usage tables copying
        country, region and org_type from it afterwards. One run through all
        of them is also cheaper than seven runs, since an address looked up
        for one table is not looked up again for the others naming it.

   -f   Reach the records an older version filled in, not only the ones it
        left empty.

        Without -f a usage record is taken only when it has no region at
        all, and an ipinfo record only when it is marked incomplete
        (stat_flag 'M'). That misses everything a version with a bug in it
        wrote and then marked done.

        With -f, a usage record is taken when its region or country is
        missing, or its org_type is '-' (nothing was known about the host),
        and an ipinfo record when it carries no org_name (ipinfo never
        answered for it, that being the only source of org_name) or its
        hostname is still the bare address (no reverse lookup ever
        succeeded). Those records are then looked up again from scratch
        rather than taken as final.

        Fix table ipinfo FIRST. The usage tables copy country, region and
        org_type from it, so correcting them against an ipinfo record that
        is itself still wrong only writes the same wrong values back.

   One of the following time-range options is required:

   -d Dates
        Fix records for the given dates.
        Date format: YYYY-MM-DD (e.g. 2024-01-15).

   -m YearMonths
        Fix records for the given months.
        Month format: YYYY-MM (e.g. 2024-01).

   -N NumberDays
        Fix records from the most recent NumberDays days.

   -y Years
        Fix records for the given years.
        Year format: YYYY (e.g. 2024).

   -b   Run in background mode (log to file only, no terminal output).

 EXAMPLE:
   fillipinfo -t wuser -y 2024
   fillipinfo -f -t all -y 2026           # every table, in the order they need

   fillipinfo -f -t ipinfo -y 2026        # or one at a time, ipinfo first
   fillipinfo -f -t osdfusage -y 2026     # then the usage tables copying from it
   fillipinfo -f -t allusage -y 2026

 NOTE:
   A record written while ipinfo was answering 429 was placed by geoip2 alone
   and has no org_name. An unplaceable address was filed as UNITED.STATES
   before version 3.0.15 and as UNKNOWN after it, so the older rows cannot be
   told apart from the addresses genuinely there and -f will not find them.
